go/ast/ast.go#1 "Package ast declares the types used to represent syntax trees for Go\npackages.\n\nSyntax trees may be constructed directly, but they are typically\nproduced from Go source code by the parser; see the\n[go/parser.ParseFile] function.\n"
  D "P(p<Package ast declares the types used to represent syntax trees for Go\npackages.>)P(p<Syntax trees may be constructed directly, but they are typically\nproduced from Go source code by the parser; see the\n>d<go/parser,,ParseFile;p<go/parser.ParseFile>>p< function.>)"
go/ast/ast.go#2 "----------------------------------------------------------------------------\nInterfaces\n\nThere are 3 main classes of nodes: Expressions and type nodes,\nstatement nodes, and declaration nodes. The node names usually\nmatch the corresponding Go spec production names to which they\ncorrespond. The node fields correspond to the individual parts\nof the respective productions.\n\nAll nodes contain position information marking the beginning of\nthe corresponding source text segment; it is accessible via the\nPos accessor method. Nodes may contain additional position info\nfor language constructs where comments may be found between parts\nof the construct (typically any larger, parenthesized subpart).\nThat position information is needed to properly position comments\nwhen printing the construct.\n"
  D "P(p<----------------------------------------------------------------------------\nInterfaces>)P(p<There are 3 main classes of nodes: Expressions and type nodes,\nstatement nodes, and declaration nodes. The node names usually\nmatch the corresponding Go spec production names to which they\ncorrespond. The node fields correspond to the individual parts\nof the respective productions.>)P(p<All nodes contain position information marking the beginning of\nthe corresponding source text segment; it is accessible via the\nPos accessor method. Nodes may contain additional position info\nfor language constructs where comments may be found between parts\nof the construct (typically any larger, parenthesized subpart).\nThat position information is needed to properly position comments\nwhen printing the construct.>)"
go/ast/ast.go#3 "All node types implement the Node interface.\n"
  D "P(p<All node types implement the Node interface.>)"
go/ast/ast.go#4 "position of first character belonging to the node\n"
  D "P(p<position of first character belonging to the node>)"
go/ast/ast.go#5 "position of first character immediately after the node\n"
  D "P(p<position of first character immediately after the node>)"
go/ast/ast.go#6 "All expression nodes implement the Expr interface.\n"
  D "P(p<All expression nodes implement the Expr interface.>)"
go/ast/ast.go#7 "All statement nodes implement the Stmt interface.\n"
  D "P(p<All statement nodes implement the Stmt interface.>)"
go/ast/ast.go#8 "All declaration nodes implement the Decl interface.\n"
  D "P(p<All declaration nodes implement the Decl interface.>)"
go/ast/ast.go#9 "----------------------------------------------------------------------------\nComments\n"
  D "P(p<----------------------------------------------------------------------------\nComments>)"
go/ast/ast.go#10 "A Comment node represents a single //-style or /*-style comment.\n\nThe Text field contains the comment text without carriage returns (\\r) that\nmay have been present in the source. Because a comment's end position is\ncomputed using len(Text), the position reported by [Comment.End] does not match the\ntrue source end position for comments containing carriage returns.\n"
